    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • 1971 Nissan Skyline 2000GT-R Is Now an 833-Piece Mattel Brick Shop Set That Can Be Customized\nHave You Seen One? 1960 Dodge Polara Hardtop Wagon Is Probably the Finest Still Around\n10 photos\nPhoto: Charles Phoenix/YouTube\nBuilt from 1960 to 1973, the Polara is not one of Dodge's longest-running nameplates. However, it's one of the brand's most iconic. But not all iterations of the premium model get attention from collectors nowadays.\nThe 1962-1964 version has a solid following today. The Polara produced during this period competed in the mid-size segment. It was among the first Mopars produced on the B-body platform and one of Dodge's earliest muscle cars. Chrysler had yet to launch the 426 Hemi, but the Polara was available with the equally spectacular Max Wedge V8.\nThen we have the Polara built from 1965 onward. This version was a full-size car and rode on the larger C-body platform. It was massive, stylish, and available with high-performance engines. The two-door versions are relatively sought-after, albeit not very expensive.\nAs for the early Polara produced in 1960-1961, which is also a full-size model, it's largely ignored. And that's mainly due to the polarizing design. Early 1960s Dodges are widely regarded as ugly, although some find them rather bold. I actually like these cars, and I'm particularly fond of the 1960 variant.\nAnd that's precisely why I'm showing you this station wagon. This and the fact that it's also a rare sight. Not necessarily by production numbers, but because many of them have morphed into rust buckets in junkyards and barns . Unlike its siblings, this grocery-getter is in pristine condition.\n1960 was the first year for the Polara. It replaced the Coronet and topped a full-size car lineup that also included the entry-level Dart and the mid-range Matador. Much like its predecessor, the Polara came in various shapes, including a four-door sedan, a four-door hardtop, a two-door hardtop, and a two-door convertible.\nHowever, Dodge also offered a rather quirky four-door hardtop station wagon. Yup, for a short period of time, some automakers experimented with pillarless grocery-getters. The hardtop version was exclusive to the Polara line; it was not available on the Dart and Matador.\nThe Polara was somewhat popular in 1960, moving 42,517 units (11% of the entire Dodge production output). However, the hardtop wagon didn't find many customers: only 3,004 examples rolled off the assembly line. This figure included 1,236 six-passenger cars and 1,768 nine-passenger (three-row) vehicles.\nThe main culprit for the low sales was the asking price. The six-passenger version started from $3,506 ($40,300 in 2026 dollars), a 30% premium over the standard Dart Seneca wagon. It was also about 9% pricier than the Matador. The nine-passenger version came in at $3,620 but could cost as much as $4,200 ($48,300 in 2026 dollars) with certain options.\nThis wagon is one of the 1,768 three-row models sold that year, and it's downright stunning. It's unclear if it's a restoration or a well-maintained survivor, but it doesn't matter all that much. The fact that it looks showroom-new is both rare and outstanding. And make sure you check them angled rear wings. While it may not be beautiful in the conventional sense, this Polara is pretty flamboyant.\nHow many of these wagons are still around? Well, I've only seen a couple cross the auction block in recent years. And back in 2020, a pristine example that was estimated to sell for $40,000 to $55,000 fetched a whopping $93,500. Hit the play button below to check this one out.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nclassic car station wagon Dodge Polara 1960 dodge polara Dodge Mopar\nAbout the author: Ciprian Florea\nAsk Ciprian about cars and he'll reveal an obsession with classics and an annoyance with modern design cues. Read his articles and you'll understand why his ideal SUV is the 1969 Chevrolet K5 Blazer. Full profile\nUP NEXT Ford CEO Tried To Buy a Ferrari Dino at an Auction and Lost",
